eystone pipeline through your  property you actually said hey you know  you could run this through the western  part of my property  I wouldn't charge you anything for that  just that would be where I would prefer  you run this and you were told basically  you can't tell us where to run the  pipeline that's that's very that's very  correct that the situation was was if  when I was under the impression at the  time that it was a crude oil pipeline  realized in Texas law and realizing that  I would have been beating my head  against the wall went ahead and said  look the route you want to take is  inappropriate it's not acceptable it's  gonna impact myself and my neighbors  I've got a solution for you there is a  misting right away with three pipelines  on it one is a small natural gas  pipeline the other is a Sunoco crude oil  pipeline I'm about a 24 inch line the  other is an abandoned had been abandoned  for nearly 30 years it was a BP Amoco  crude oil line all they had to do was  come in a straight line on the western  edge of my property follow the route  that was an existing oil route right  away that was already there  and in the end the problem would have  been solved and I told them in I said I  do not want your money I don't like you  I don't like your company I will give  you the land just stay away from where I  live we shook hands we had a meeting we  shook hands they agreed to it but the  man told me and no Sun no uncertain  terms they they cannot be told by any  regulatory agency or land on earth where  they can put their line they can put  their line directly under your house and  under Texas law they have to they have  to pay for the house to be moved well  that just gives them unlimited authority  and I'm opposed to that hey Michael is  it common in Texas to have three  pipelines going through your property as  it is and then I mean is these common  situations where people have so much  stuff going through their properties oh  absolutely you're you're looking at a  state that it's just there's millions of  miles of pipelines ladyness in this  state and  and basically it's their every word you  can't go three or four hundred feet not  find a pipeline somewhere so yeah it's  very common as it stands right now  what's the latest offer because I know  there have been a number of  back-and-forth sand discussions with  TransCanada about the Keystone XL  pipeline going through your property  what's the latest offer that you have on  the table or where do things stand right  now the latest off we've settled we  settled in a mediation hearing but that  settlement as I've contended in my  lawsuits was done under coercion and  extreme duress and I can prove that  beyond a shadow of a doubt it's just  it's ridiculous and TransCanada even  though I've settled I've gone back to  court and filed a fraud lawsuit I filed  a suit in Travis County with the state  capital Austin Texas against the  Railroad Commission which is the  permitting agency and I'm I'm halfway  through preparing my suit against the  railroad I mean the Army Corps of  Engineers  so are there is work being done on your  property right now the work is just  completed although the property has not  been restored as as they agree to and  they're originals in the original  settlement or cording to the law they  have not they have not restored the  property and in all honesty the damage  is irreparable they will never be able  to restore the property to its original  okay so that's what I want to focus on  what's been done what damage has been  done  I've got it I've got two creeks going  through my property and these creature  net are a springfed natural creeks and  the problem is they have muddied those  creeks at the point they've destroyed  the vegetation the wildlife they have  destroyed the banks of the creek they  came in with their giant excavators and  track codes and they they leveled and  leveled the creek the bank of the creek  down to the point where it will never be  restored to its natural condition never  hey I'm curious Michael what before this  incident right what were your politics  like in other words were you already  what was your view on oil and drilling  has this changed it or it has it just so  happened that they came across somebody  who was against this stuff to begin with  look I've spent a large amount of my  years in in the petrochemical industry I  was a firefighter/paramedic I worked as  a lab technician and eventually as a  chemist and I was raised on the ship  channel in Houston  I have no I'm not I have no beef with  oil oil is not the problem this is not  crude oil as my lawsuit or content or a  difference here the problem is and you  know we're going back to your question  I'm I'm an ultraconservative basically I  voted Republican since Richard Nixon  sucked in the marine corps in the 60s  but now I am a full-blood libertarian so  and basically this is a about I used to  belong to a group of scientists that  believe that this this climate change  claim was a fabrication it was an  economic manipulation I have since  changed my views on that I am now a  member of the American Union of  Concerned Scientists fascinating and and  so I'm guessing that you're okay you  know this you your story was pitched to  me by a pretty progressive you know  liberal organization you see you're okay  on those issues working with progressive  groups I would take it absolutely listen  here's the thing climate change is not a  fantasy this is a real event we're in  we're in serious trouble in this country  and then people like me had to wake up a  hard way and say no wait a minute I'm a  very I'm an analytical chemist I have to  stop and I have to look at the facts I  have to assess those facts and I think  the evidence is overwhelming at this  point that we we have adjust our thought  process but going back to a local  personal issue this is a matter of  eminent domain and sovereignty of  American citizens you're talking about a  foreign private corporation stealing  American land for private personal gain  and I'm sick and tired of my co-workers  sitting here telling me that  oh this is good it's going to lower the  price of gasoline it's going to give us  security for America and its baloney  you're talking about a foreign private  corporation located in Canada it's  bringing down a toxic corrosive material  that is not crude oil by any definition  federal or state and this and taking it  to another foreign private corporation  that corporation is motiva one of them  is motiva motiva is a joint venture  between Royal Dutch Shell and Saudi  Arabia refining that is a private  economic gain and Texas law they are  violating Texas law the Constitution  clearly states that in our state your  property can only be taken for public  use not for private economic day so I'm  fighting them and we're gonna win and  they're gonna have to dig the damn line  up period all right we've been speaking  with Michael Bishop from Texas Michael  really great to hear your story I'm  sorry that this is the reality of the  situation but we're gonna keep following  it and we'll definitely have you back  David thank you for having me I  appreciate

